{"name":"Glass Pebble","hex":"#dbd8cb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"glass","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An amorphous solid, often transparent substance, usually made by melting silica sand with various additives (for most purposes, a mixture of soda, potash and lime is added).","Any amorphous solid (one without a regular crystal lattice)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fit with glass; to glaze.","To enclose in glass."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glass"},{"term":"pebble","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small stone, especially one rounded by the action of water.","A particle from 4 to 64 mm in diameter, following the Wentworth scale."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To pave with pebbles.","To deposit water droplets on the ice."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pebble"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glass","blocks":[{"text":"Glass is an amorphous (non-crystalline) solid. Because it is often transparent and chemically inert, glass has found widespread practical, technological, and decorative use in window panes, tableware, and optics. Some common objects made of glass are named after the material, e.g., a \"glass\" for drinking, \"glasses\" for vision correction, and a \"magnifying glass\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg/330px-Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass","wikidata":"Q11469"},{"title":"Pebble","blocks":[{"text":"A pebble is a clast of rock with a particle size of 4–64 mm (0.16–2.52 in) based on the Udden-Wentworth scale of sedimentology. Pebbles are generally considered larger than granules and smaller than cobbles. Pebble tools are among the earliest known man-made artifacts, dating from the Palaeolithic period of human history.